WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.105
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
What's wrong, Martin?

00:00:02.105 --> 00:00:03.240
Having trouble choosing?

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.115
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:00:04.115 --> 00:00:06.700
I just can't decide
which tool I want to use.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.720
I don't know if this new hammer
will do everything I need.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.850
And I don't want to
throw away my old mallet

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:12.830
without being sure.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.580
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Well,
what if I told you

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.370
that you didn't have to choose
right now and you can use both?

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.810
MARTIN OMANDER:
That sounds great.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.923
Stay tuned if you
want to know more.

00:00:20.923 --> 00:00:24.374
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.990
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Liz
works for StoreCraft

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.200
as a site reliability engineer,
where she's on call maintaining

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.720
their custom storefront.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.400
StoreCraft's systems are
developed in-house in Python

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.520
and have been running on virtual
machines for years, a set up

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.610
which evolved as
StoreCraft's businesses did.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:47.860
These virtual machines use
slightly different versions

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:48.670
of Python.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.330
This is causing extra pain
and work for Liz and her team.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.430
Liz is looking for a
way to reduce this pain

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.610
by updating all the
components to current Python

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.340
and also migrating away
from virtual machines

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.250
onto something more
scalable, like serverless.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:06.100
MARTIN OMANDER: Liz knows that
this upgrade will take time.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.950
And her team needs to make
sure the existing system keeps

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:10.000
running.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.310
She realizes that she
can best achieve this

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.350
by completing the
upgrade in two steps.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.640
First, moving to serverless,
then making the language

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:19.502
upgrades.

00:01:19.502 --> 00:01:21.460
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: StoreCraft
is about to launch

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.320
a new recommendation
engine, which

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.470
is written using Python 3.9.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.540
Liz sees this as the
perfect candidate

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.480
to deploy a serverless first,
skipping the virtual machine

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:32.423
altogether.

00:01:32.423 --> 00:01:34.840
MARTIN OMANDER: Looking at the
options available on Google

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.930
Cloud, Liz sees that
while she could deploy

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.120
on Cloud Functions,
she would be forced

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.480
to use only specific
versions of Python.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.940
However, she sees that
the newest serverless

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.940
offering from Google
Cloud, Cloud Run,

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.630
doesn't have this limitation.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:53.680
So she chooses to
deploy there instead.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.020
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Creating
this service on Cloud Run,

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.720
Liz has complete control
over the container.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.350
Making use of the official
Python images on Docker hub,

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.360
she can pick the exact
version of Python

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.190
she needs down to
the patch level

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:09.130
by specifying the base image
in the front line of the Docker

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:09.940
file.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.880
As each Cloud Run service is
based on its own container

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.820
image, which are each defined
by their own Docker file,

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.980
Liz has complete control
over the languages

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.657
used in each part of her setup.

00:02:19.657 --> 00:02:21.490
MARTIN OMANDER: Since
each Cloud Run service

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:25.030
has this level of isolation,
Liz can move each of them

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:28.090
to Cloud Run one at a time.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.580
She is also able to maintain
the services running

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.820
on the original virtual
machines until the team has

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.693
the availability to upgrade.

00:02:35.693 --> 00:02:37.610
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: However,
during the refactor

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.230
of the account
services component,

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:41.110
StoreCraft's
development team finds

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:44.170
that it has a dependency on
the sweet-ldap package, which

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.450
doesn't yet support Python 3.

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.940
Liz is able to keep
the service running on

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:53.230
the now deprecated Python
2.7 by specifying the latest

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.930
version of Python 2.7
in the Docker file.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.170
As soon as the sweet-ldap
package has Python 3 support,

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:02.620
Liz can test this new version
by gradually rolling out

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.440
a new revision of the service
using the traffic splitting

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.210
feature of Cloud Run.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.610
She can set the new revision
of the count service

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:12.580
to, say, service
only 5% of visitors,

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.470
that way only some
of her customers

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:15.970
end up using the new code.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.580
If she notices that something
isn't working as intended,

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.190
she can easily roll
back to the old version.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.980
MARTIN OMANDER: By migrating
each service in turn,

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.430
Liz is able to control
them in isolation, allowing

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:30.310
for a more incremental
upgrade process and a more

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:31.870
reliable environment.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.120
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: But Martin,
I will have to take away

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.910
that mallet at some point.

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:40.090
MARTIN OMANDER: OK then.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.280
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: You
still can have your hammer.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.440
MARTIN OMANDER: Well, I
do like this new hammer,

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.207
now that I'm used to it.

00:03:46.207 --> 00:03:47.207
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Great.

00:03:47.207 --> 00:03:48.580
[THUDDING]

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.080
Great.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

